Most of what you think is “you” isn’t.

Not your opinions. Not your fears. Not your interpretations of why someone didn’t text back. Most of it was installed before you had a chance to choose.

I learned this the hard way.

## **The Loss You Don’t Notice**

A few years ago, someone I cared about stopped talking to me. Not dramatically. Just slowly. Messages got shorter. Responses came later. Eventually, nothing.

I told myself it was them. They were distant. They were going through something. They’d reach out when they were ready.

I was right about one thing: they were going through something. A parent’s illness. Financial stress. The kind of quiet emergency people don’t broadcast.

What I didn’t see was that my response made it worse. The matching distance. The refusal to ask. The quiet certainty that I was the one being wronged.

They read my silence as rejection. I read theirs as abandonment.

Two people, both certain, both wrong.

![](/uploads/posts/is-this-mine-or-is-this-borrowed/image.png)

By the time I understood what had happened, the window was closed. Not with a fight. Not with a moment you can point to. Just a door that had been open — and now wasn’t.

That’s what bias costs when you don’t catch it. Not arguments. Absences.

## **The Crack**

The shift didn’t come from a big idea. It came from something stupid: breakfast.

*Three square meals.*

Why three? Why square? Who decided that was the correct way to eat — and why did it survive long enough to feel obvious to me?

That question shouldn’t matter. But it does. Because if something that basic was inherited, repeated, and internalised until it felt like “just how things are” — then what else is? Beliefs about silence. About respect. About what it means when someone pulls away. About when to speak and when to stay quiet. About what you deserve.

By the time you ask “who am I?”, most of the answer is already installed. You don’t start from zero. You start from inheritance.

## **Why Awareness Isn’t Enough**

Psychologist Emily Pronin calls this the bias blind spot: the belief that everyone else is biased, but you see things clearly (Pronin, Lin, & Ross, 2002). You can spot your friend’s confirmation bias from across the room, but your own certainty feels like truth.

I knew about the bias blind spot. I just didn’t think I had it. Not until that door closed.

Here’s what I thought while that door was closing: that I was being patient. That I was giving them space. That I was protecting my dignity. I thought I was seeing clearly. I thought I was being objective.

I was wrong. But in the moment, it felt like truth. That’s what bias does. It doesn’t feel like bias. It feels like clarity.

And that’s the problem. Most conversations about bias stop at awareness. “We’re all biased. Be more mindful.” That changes nothing. I was aware. I still lost someone. Knowing you’re wrong doesn’t stop you from feeling right. And feeling right is what drives the decision.

![](/uploads/posts/is-this-mine-or-is-this-borrowed/image.png)

## **The Mechanism**

There’s a part of you that decides before you finish thinking. It reads silence as rejection. Distance as threat. Difference as danger.

It’s fast. It’s efficient. And most of the time, it’s useful. It kept our ancestors alive.

The problem is that this system doesn’t know the difference between a real threat and a tired human being going through something they don’t know how to explain. So it fills in the gap. And it always fills it in with a story that protects you — even if that story costs you the relationship.

![](/uploads/posts/is-this-mine-or-is-this-borrowed/4d799e44-1a5e-44d8-8d09-8622fe78d1be.png)

Knowing this doesn’t switch it off. But it creates a moment. And a moment is enough.

## **What This Looks Like in Practice**

If you actually use this idea, here’s one way it can unfold. Not perfectly. Not consistently. But in sequence.

***Day 1–2: You catch the flash.***

You’re in an argument. You feel the spike — annoyance, certainty, the story forming. You don’t know what to do with it yet. But you notice it. That’s new.

***Day 3–4: You start categorising.***

A friend doesn’t text back. The old story rises:&nbsp;*they’re ignoring me.*&nbsp;But now you ask: is this interpretation mine, or is this borrowed? You don’t know the answer. But the question stops you from sending that passive-aggressive message.

***Day 5–6: You trace one belief.***

You’re about to turn down an opportunity. The voice says: “don’t do past yourself.” You pause and ask: whose voice is that? You realise it’s your father’s — a good man who played small his whole life. You take the opportunity anyway. Not because you’re sure it’s right. Because you’re no longer sure the fear was yours.

***Day 7: You have one conversation differently.***

Someone disagrees with you. The old pattern is to argue, to prove, to win. But you’ve been practising the question. You say: “I don’t get it. Help me understand what I’m missing.” They pause. They talk. You listen.

Nothing resolves. But you stayed in the room.

That’s what change looks like. Not enlightenment. Just a few moments where you don’t automatically pilot your way out of a relationship.

## **One Interruption**

There’s no system for this. Just one question.

When you feel the spike — pause. Three seconds.

Ask:&nbsp;*Is this mine, or is this borrowed?*

![](/uploads/posts/is-this-mine-or-is-this-borrowed/image.png)

Is this emotion mine? Is this belief mine? Is this interpretation mine? Or did I inherit this way of seeing without ever choosing it?

The question doesn’t give you an answer. It does something more important: it slows you down before you act on something you didn’t examine.

## **What Changes**

Nothing about this makes you objective. You don’t become neutral. You don’t suddenly see everything clearly.

If anything, it gets more uncomfortable. Because now you’re aware of how much of what felt like you wasn’t yours.

But you gain something more useful than clarity: hesitation. And hesitation — at the right moment — is what keeps you from turning a misunderstanding into a loss.

## **What Remains**

Most relationships don’t end in conflict. They end in interpretation. In stories both people were sure were true.

You won’t notice when you get it wrong. You’ll only notice what’s no longer there.

So the next time you’re certain — about a silence, a tone, a delay, a shift — pause. Not to become objective. Just long enough to question the certainty.

> *Is this mine? Or is this borrowed?*

You won’t always get a better answer. But sometimes, you’ll stay in the room long enough to find out what was actually there.

\**

*The bias blind spot was first described by Emily Pronin, Lin, and Ross in a 2002 paper in Personality and Social Psychology Bulletin.*
